**AI Maze Escape (BFS)**

AI Maze Escape Logic

Represent the maze as a 2D grid.
Treat each empty cell as a node.
Start from the START position.
Use Breadth First Search (BFS) to explore all possible paths.
Visit neighboring cells (Up, Down, Left, Right).
Ignore walls and already visited cells.
Stop when the GOAL is reached.
Trace back the path to obtain the shortest route.

## Pseudocode

```text
START

Initialize queue
Enqueue START node
Mark START as visited

WHILE queue is not empty

    Dequeue current node

    IF current node is GOAL
        Display shortest path
        STOP

    FOR each neighboring cell (Up, Down, Left, Right)

        IF neighbor is inside maze
           AND neighbor is not a wall
           AND neighbor is not visited

            Mark neighbor as visited
            Store parent of neighbor
            Enqueue neighbor

END WHILE

END
```

**N-Queens (Backtracking)**

N-Queens Challenge Logic

 Place one queen in each row.
 Before placing a queen, check whether the position is safe.
 A position is safe if no queen exists in the same column or diagonals.
 If the position is safe, place the queen.
 Continue placing queens in the next row.
 If no safe position is found, remove the previous queen (backtracking).
 Repeat until all queens are placed successfully.

## Pseudocode

```text
START

FUNCTION Solve(row)

    IF row == N
        Display board
        RETURN TRUE

    FOR each column

        IF position is safe

            Place Queen

            IF Solve(row + 1)
                RETURN TRUE

            Remove Queen

    RETURN FALSE

CALL Solve(0)

END
```

**Water Jug Puzzle (BFS)**

Water Jug Puzzle Logic

 Start with both jugs empty.
 Generate all possible actions:
   - Fill a jug.
   - Empty a jug.
   - Pour water from one jug to another.
 Represent each water level as a state.
 Use Breadth First Search (BFS) to explore all possible states.
 Avoid revisiting previously explored states.
 Continue until exactly 8 litres is obtained in one jug.
 Display the sequence of actions taken.

## Pseudocode

```text
START

Create queue
Insert initial state (0,0)

WHILE queue is not empty

    Remove current state

    IF target amount is found
        Display solution
        STOP

    Generate all possible next states

        Fill Jug1
        Fill Jug2
        Empty Jug1
        Empty Jug2
        Pour Jug1 → Jug2
        Pour Jug2 → Jug1

    Add unvisited states to queue

END WHILE

END
```

**Connect Four (Minimax)**

Connect Four Logic

 Create an empty 6×7 game board.
 The human player makes the first move.
 The AI evaluates all valid moves.
 The AI selects the best move using the Minimax algorithm with Alpha-Beta Pruning.
 Alternate turns between the player and the AI.
 After every move, check for four consecutive pieces horizontally, vertically, or diagonally.
 If a player connects four pieces, declare the winner.
 If the board is full without a winner, declare a draw.

## Pseudocode

```text
START

Create empty game board

WHILE game is not over

    Player makes a move

    IF player wins
        Display "Player Wins"
        STOP

    AI evaluates all valid moves

    Apply Minimax with Alpha-Beta Pruning

    AI chooses the best move

    IF AI wins
        Display "AI Wins"
        STOP

    IF board is full
        Display "Draw"
        STOP

END WHILE

END
```

**8-Puzzle (A* Search)**

8-Puzzle AI Challenge Logic

 Represent the puzzle as a 3×3 board.
 Treat the blank tile (0) as the movable space.
 Generate all possible moves of the blank tile.
 Use the A* Search algorithm to find the optimal solution.
 Calculate:
   - g(n): Cost from the start state.
   - h(n): Manhattan Distance heuristic.
   - f(n) = g(n) + h(n).
 Select the state with the smallest f(n).
 Repeat until the goal state is reached.
 Display the sequence of moves and the minimum number of moves required.

## Pseudocode

```text
START

Create OPEN list
Insert initial state

WHILE OPEN list is not empty

    Select state with lowest f(n)

    IF state is GOAL
        Display solution path
        STOP

    Generate all possible moves

    Calculate

        g(n) = Cost from start
        h(n) = Manhattan Distance
        f(n) = g(n) + h(n)

    Insert new states into OPEN list

END WHILE

END
```
